  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2018-12-21T15:05:59.593Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "After the new update it seems that quite a fair amount of people aren't too happy with the performance ranking system. As it stands, it is a fairly poor system that needs quite a lot of improvements, which is understandable as it's in it's first iteration. I think one of the very biggest problems is that fact that one thing can set your avatar to very poor. For example: If you have over 70k pollies then you're avatar would be very poor, but also if you have: 60403 dynamic bones, 525 colliders, 1000 materials, you will also be ranked as very poor. \nTo combat this issue I feel like a points system, where the user is given points depending on which ranking each component category gets given, would be a step in the right direction into improving the system. Then in the future, this could possibly be expanded on where the different categories give you more or less points depending on how expensive their performance is, for example, having 500 dynamic bones would be much worse as having over 10 trail renderers (Both are ranked as very poor). \n\nAs a base line this is how it would be:\n\nWhen your avatar has a component that is ranked at the different performance rankings, it gives you a certain amount of performance points, the more performance points you have, the better optimised you are and gives you your overall ranking\n\nExcellent = 5 points\nGood = 4 Points\nMedium = 2 points\nPoor = 1 Points\nVery Poor = 0 Points\n\nThen to actually achieve overall excellent you need something like 45 - 50 points\nGood = 30 - 45 points\nMedium = 25 - 30 points\nPoor = 25 points\nVery Poor = 0 - 25 points\n\nThese numbers however will likely need to be changed as this is just from the top of my head, but it sets a base of how the performance ranks would be.\n\nHopefully this actually gets taken into consideration as like I mentioned, I do think it would be a good step into improving the system and making it better",
  "error": null,
